{"about":"9843f16c55a02ce422d04eb9057d52cb554dfe0052e19a3d19653ba96e18dc92","body":"By accepting that corroboration offers no epistemic justification that a theory is likely true, the conjecture reduces scientific progress to a blind gamble where the 'best' theory is merely the one that has not yet failed, rather than the one most aligned with reality. This costs science its claim to approximate truth (verisimilitude) in any meaningful sense, as it severs the logical link between surviving tests and the likelihood of the theory being correct. If we cannot say a well-corroborated theory is more likely to be true than an untested one, the preference for it becomes an arbitrary psychological habit rather than a defensible rational stance, undermining the objective status of scientific knowledge.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The cost of abandoning epistemic justification"}